{"term":"conjunction","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:6a01f85dbb96b28a0d","definition":"The act of joining, or condition of being joined.","example":"[…] Dr. Minchin in return was quite sure that man was not a mere machine or a fortuitous conjunction of atoms; […]","pos":"noun","synonyms":["connection","union"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["comparing conjunction","conjuncatenation","conjunctional","conjunction fallacy","conjunctionless","contrasting conjunction","coordinating conjunction","in conjunction with","misconjunction","nonconjunction","subordinating conjunction"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/conjunction#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:d6604f50ad05a170ec","definition":"A word used to join other words, phrases, or clauses together into sentences. (The specific conjunction used shows how the two joined parts are related semantically.)","example":"A comma is placed between short members of compound sentences, connected by and, but, for, nor, or, because, whereas, that expressing purpose (so that, in order that), and other conjunctions.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable, uncountable","wordFamily":["comparing conjunction","conjuncatenation","conjunctional","conjunction fallacy","conjunctionless","contrasting conjunction","coordinating conjunction","in conjunction with","misconjunction","nonconjunction","subordinating conjunction"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/conjunction#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:d21c2602302ddcdc22","definition":"Cooccurrence; coincidence.","example":"[…] the coexistence of one such phenomenon with another; or the succession of one such phenomenon to another: their conjunction, in s